// shader-differential - texture-binding control, arithmetic twin.
//
// The base procedural texture is 64x64 A8R8G8B8 with texel (x, y) =
// (R, G, B, A) = (4x, 4(63 - y), 4(63 - x), 4y) in 8-bit units - four
// DISTINCT channel functions.  Every sampler is bound to that texture
// with its channels PERMUTED by the sampler's NAME (guest auto_tex_perm =
// FNV-1a(name) mod 24 into the lexicographic RGBA table; the stager
// prints it per sampler as "texture <row> <name> = perm N (XXXX)").
// The sampled twin's sampler is named u_tex, whose permutation is
// 6 (GRBA): output R reads base G, output G reads base R, B and A stay.
// So the texel u_tex sees is (4(63 - y), 4x, 4(63 - x), 4y).
//
// At RT pixel (px, py) the interpolated texcoord is ((px + 0.5)/64,
// (py + 0.5)/64), so floor(uv * 64) recovers (px, py) with half a texel
// of margin, and the nearest-sampled texel is the same (px, py).  4/255
// scaling makes every channel exactly representable after the RT's 8-bit
// quantisation.
//
// This twin is therefore ALSO the proof that the host's and the guest's
// permutation tables agree for at least one name: rename u_tex, or change
// either table, and this row goes red until the twin is rewritten for the
// new permutation.

void main(
    float4 in_texcoord   : TEXCOORD0,
    out float4 out_color : COLOR)
{
    float x = floor(in_texcoord.x * 64.0f);
    float y = floor(in_texcoord.y * 64.0f);
    float s = 4.0f / 255.0f;
    out_color = float4((63.0f - y) * s, x * s, (63.0f - x) * s, y * s);
}
